 ## Background
-The concept of adaptive density control was introduced in the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S8, unofficial]. The method was designed to dynamically adjust the number of Gaussians to match the complexity of the scene, addressing the limitations of static point cloud initialization. Since then, numerous variants have been proposed to improve efficiency, quality, and robustness, including score-based methods [S1, unofficial], MCMC-based approaches [S1, unofficial], and dynamic scene adaptations [S1, unofficial]. The family has expanded to include specialized strategies for SLAM [S1, unofficial], compression [S2], and neural network-based refinement [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of adaptive densification originated with the introduction of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1, S8, unofficial]. The initial implementation used a heuristic based on 2D positional gradient magnitudes to split or clone Gaussians, addressing the limitations of static point cloud representations. Subsequent developments have diversified this family, introducing probabilistic approaches like MCMC [S1, unofficial], budget-constrained methods like Score-based Densification [S1, unofficial], and efficiency-focused variants like Trick-GS [S3] and Mini-Splatting [S2]. These methods are widely used in novel view synthesis, SLAM, and dynamic scene reconstruction.
 
